/-!
# Discrete valuation rings unramified over a discrete valuation ring

A local ring which is flat, essentially of finite type and formally unramified over a discrete
valuation ring `A` is again a discrete valuation ring, and a uniformizer of `A` is a uniformizer
of it. A ring covered by such subalgebras, over which `A` maps locally, is a discrete valuation
ring as well.

Let `A` be a discrete valuation ring with fraction field `K` and let `L / K` be a finite separable
extension. The integral closure `B` of `A` in `L` is a finite Noetherian `A`-algebra of dimension
one and a Dedekind domain; its maximal ideals `m_i` all lie over the maximal ideal of `A`, the
local rings `B_{m_i}` are discrete valuation rings, and the degree of `L / K` is the sum of the
products `e_i f_i` of the ramification indices and the residue degrees. There is exactly one such
maximal ideal when `A` is henselian, in particular when it is complete for its
maximal-ideal-adic topology. This lets us say when `L / K` is *unramified*, *tamely ramified* or
*totally ramified* with respect to `A`. Unramifiedness passes to subextensions, is inherited by a
finite Galois overextension and by a common overextension of two unramified extensions, and is
stable under composition in a tower.

## Main definitions

* `IsDiscreteValuationRing.IsUnramifiedWithRespectTo`: at every maximal ideal of the integral
  closure lying over the maximal ideal of `A` the ramification index is one and the residue field
  extension is separable.
* `IsDiscreteValuationRing.IsTamelyRamifiedWithRespectTo`: every residue field extension is
  separable and, in positive residue characteristic, every ramification index is prime to it.
* `IsDiscreteValuationRing.IsTotallyRamifiedWithRespectTo`: the integral closure has a unique
  maximal ideal above that of `A` and the residue field extension there is trivial.

## Main results

* `IsDiscreteValuationRing.of_forall_eq_unit_mul_pow`: a ring with a nonunit `π`, no power of which
  vanishes, in which every nonzero element is a unit times a power of `π`, is a discrete valuation
  ring with uniformizer `π`.
* `IsLocalRing.isDiscreteValuationRing_of_maximalIdeal_eq_span`: a Noetherian local ring whose
  maximal ideal is generated by a nonzerodivisor is a discrete valuation ring.
* `IsDiscreteValuationRing.of_formallyUnramified`: a local ring flat, essentially of finite type
  and formally unramified over a discrete valuation ring is a discrete valuation ring.
* `IsDiscreteValuationRing.of_forall_mem_subalgebra`: a ring covered by subalgebras as in the
  previous item, with a local structure map, is a discrete valuation ring.
* `IsDiscreteValuationRing.integralClosure_finite_dedekind_and_degree_eq_sum_ramification_inertia`:
  the structure of the integral closure of `A` in a finite separable extension of `K`, the
  fundamental degree identity, and uniqueness of the prime above a henselian base.
* `IsDiscreteValuationRing.ramificationIdx_eq_finrank_of_totallyRamified`: the ramification index
  of a totally ramified finite separable extension equals its degree.
* `isUnramifiedWithRespectTo_of_tower_and_exists_isGalois_and_common_extension`, in
  `IsDiscreteValuationRing`: the three permanence properties of unramified extensions with
  respect to `A`.
* `IsDiscreteValuationRing.isUnramifiedIn_comp`: unramifiedness over a prime composes in a tower.

## Implementation notes

The results producing a discrete valuation ring are stated as the conjunction of `IsDomain` and
`IsDiscreteValuationRing`, since the latter takes the former as an instance argument.

The finitely many maximal ideals of the integral closure are represented by a duplicate-free
family indexed by `Fin n`; ramification indices and residue degrees use Mathlib's canonical
`Ideal.ramificationIdx` and `Ideal.inertiaDeg`, while the ramification predicates use
`Ideal.ramificationIdx'` and the residue extension supplied by
`Ideal.Quotient.algebraOfLiesOver`. Completeness means maximal-ideal-adic completeness. The tame
and total predicates are transparent abbreviations of `Stacks.KummerExtension.IsTamelyRamified` and
`Stacks.KummerExtension.IsTotallyRamified`. Containment of field extensions is represented by
  compatible
algebra structures and scalar towers; the common overextension is not required to be a specified
compositum, in accordance with the source. Unramifiedness in a tower is stated for an arbitrary
tower of commutative rings and a prime of the base: applying it to the integral closures and the
maximal ideal of `A` gives the source statement, since
`Algebra.isUnramifiedAt_iff_map_eq` identifies the local condition in `Algebra.IsUnramifiedIn`
with ramification index one together with separability of the residue field extension.

